<main>
  <section class="hero bg-dark">
    <div class="wrap hero-center">
      <span class="eyebrow on-dark-pill"><span class="dot"></span>SHOWED UP EMPTY-HANDED? GRAB ONE</span>
      <h1>Four ready-to-build projects.<br><em>Pick one, jump straight in.</em></h1>
      <p class="lede">Each project is pre-loaded: a persona, a downloadable source material file, a custom-instructions template, a paste-ready RCI brief, critique prompts, and an acceptance checklist. No prep needed — just pick, download, and go.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="tabs-wrap">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
        <button class="tab-btn active" data-target="0"><span class="num">01</span>Portfolio</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-target="1"><span class="num">02</span>Pitch Deck</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-target="2"><span class="num">03</span>Dashboard</button>
        <button class="tab-btn" data-target="3"><span class="num">04</span>One-Pager</button>
      </div>

      <!-- PANEL 01 — PORTFOLIO -->
      <div class="panel active" data-panel="0">
        <div class="wrap" style="padding:0;">
          <div class="persona-head">
            <div>
              <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>DIRECTOR PERSONA</span>
              <h2>Sarah Chen — Marketing Director</h2>
              <p>12 years in B2B SaaS and consumer tech. Builds a one-page portfolio that gets a hiring manager or client to reach out.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Source Material</span>
                <h3>Who you're building for</h3>
                <p>Marketing director leading demand gen at a Series C SaaS company (~200 employees). Known for programs that are measurable and tied to revenue, not vanity metrics.</p>
                <p><strong>Five sample projects loaded as source:</strong></p>
                <ul>
                  <li>Demand gen overhaul — 65% higher conversion to opportunity, $2.1M attributed pipeline</li>
                  <li>Content engine build — 10K → 400K monthly visitors in 18 months</li>
                  <li>Enterprise product launch — 15 opportunities in 30 days</li>
                  <li>ABM pilot — 1 closed deal worth $180K ARR, adopted as ongoing strategy</li>
                  <li>Marketing ops infrastructure — real-time pipeline visibility for leadership</li>
                </ul>
                <a class="download-btn" href="/GHN/AIFluency/week-2/source-material/01-portfolio-source-material.md" download="portfolio-source-material.md">⬇ Download source material</a>
                <p class="download-note">Drag the downloaded file straight into your co-work project.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Custom Instructions</span>
                <div class="code-block">
                  <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="ci-01">Copy</button>
                  <pre id="ci-01">You are my collaborator on Demo Portfolio — Director Persona.

My role: Marketing Director at a mid-size SaaS company
What we're building: a one-page portfolio site
Success looks like: a hiring manager or potential client can understand my work and impact in 30 seconds of scanning

Tone: warm-professional, peer-level, no jargon. Confident but not braggy.
Push back when my brief is vague.
Always ask for the missing context rather than guessing.
Save artifacts to /Demo Portfolio/ so I can find them later.
When iterating, surface what you changed and why — don't just hand me a new draft.</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">RCI Brief — paste-ready</span>
                <div class="code-block">
                  <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="rci-01">Copy</button>
                  <pre id="rci-01">I need to build a one-page portfolio site for a marketing director.

Requirements:
- Clean, scannable layout — a hiring manager should get the picture in 30 seconds
- Hero section with name, title, and one-line value proposition
- 3-5 featured projects with one-line impact statements (not paragraphs)
- A short bio section (3-4 sentences, not a resume)
- Contact section with email and LinkedIn
- No animation, no dark patterns, no stock photo placeholders
- Output as a single HTML file I can open in a browser

Context:
I'm a marketing director with 12 years in B2B SaaS and consumer tech. My source material is loaded in this project - bio, 5 projects with impact metrics, and contact info. Use it.

Intent:
A hiring manager or potential client lands on this page and immediately understands what I'm good at, what I've shipped, and wants to reach out. The page should feel like a confident professional, not a template.

Go.</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Critique Prompts — pick one</span>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When the value prop reads generic ("results-driven leader")</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"The value proposition in the hero section is generic. Rewrite it to be specific — what's the one thing she does that most marketing directors don't? Lead with that."</p>
                </div>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When projects read like a resume, not impact statements</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"The project descriptions are process-focused. Rewrite each to lead with the measurable outcome, then the role. 'Grew organic traffic 40x' not 'Managed content strategy.'"</p>
                </div>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When the layout is too dense to scan in 30 seconds</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"The page is too dense. Cut it down. Hero, 3 projects max, 3-sentence bio, contact. Nothing else."</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">What Good Looks Like</span>
                <ul class="checklist">
                  <li>Hero: name, title, one-line value prop — not a paragraph</li>
                  <li>3-5 projects, each one line, impact-first</li>
                  <li>Value proposition is specific, not generic</li>
                  <li>No jargon — no "synergy," "leverage," "best-in-class"</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="done-bar">"If I were a hiring manager and this landed in my inbox, <strong>I'd want to talk to this person</strong>." Not "it's fine."</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PANEL 02 — PITCH DECK -->
      <div class="panel" data-panel="1">
        <div class="wrap" style="padding:0;">
          <div class="persona-head">
            <div>
              <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>FOUNDER PERSONA</span>
              <h2>Marcus Rivera — Founder, Pulseboard</h2>
              <p>Ex-Stripe PM, pre-seed, 3 headcount, $180K raised, 9-month runway. Builds a 10-slide deck that earns a second investor meeting.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Source Material</span>
                <h3>Pulseboard — the one-pager</h3>
                <p><strong>Problem:</strong> engineering leaders lose 4-6 hrs/week to unrecorded status meetings. <strong>Solution:</strong> auto-transcribe and structure daily leader updates.</p>
                <p><strong>Market:</strong> ~40K mid-market eng orgs, $800/mo target, path to $10M ARR via ~1,000 teams.</p>
                <p><strong>Traction:</strong> 6 design partners, 2 paying customers ($400/mo), 14-person waitlist, NPS 71.</p>
                <p><strong>Ask:</strong> $1.5M seed — 4 engineers, extend runway to 24mo, beta by month 6.</p>
                <p><strong>3 investor personas loaded:</strong> Lin (operator-VC, wants founder quality), Devon (pre-seed specialist, wants a real wedge), Priya (strategic angel, wants a coachable founder).</p>
                <a class="download-btn" href="/GHN/AIFluency/week-2/source-material/02-pitch-deck-source-material.md" download="pitch-deck-source-material.md">⬇ Download source material</a>
                <p class="download-note">Drag the downloaded file straight into your co-work project.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Custom Instructions</span>
                <div class="code-block">
                  <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="ci-02">Copy</button>
                  <pre id="ci-02">You are my collaborator on Demo Pitch Deck — Founder Persona.

My role: First-time founder raising a seed round
What we're building: a 10-slide pitch deck outline with talking points per slide
Success looks like: a partner at a seed fund hears the pitch in a 30-minute meeting and wants a second conversation

Tone: direct, confident, board-ready. No hedging, no filler.
Push back when my brief is vague.
Always ask for the missing context rather than guessing.
Save artifacts to /Demo Pitch Deck/ so I can find them later.
When iterating, surface what you changed and why — don't just hand me a new draft.</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">RCI Brief — paste-ready</span>
                <div class="code-block">
                  <button class="btn-copy" data-copy-target="rci-02">Copy</button>
                  <pre id="rci-02">I need to build a 10-slide pitch deck outline for a pre-seed founder.

Requirements:
- Exactly 10 slides (no more, no fewer)
- Each slide: a title, a one-sentence thesis, and 2-3 talking points
- Order: title -> problem -> insight -> solution -> market -> traction -> business model -> team -> ask -> close
- The opening slide should not introduce the company — it should introduce the problem
- The "ask" slide should be specific (amount, what it funds, what it unlocks) — not "we're raising to scale"
- The close should leave room for the partner to ask a question, not be a hard pitch
- No investor jargon ("we're disrupting," "best-in-class," "revolutionary")
- Include 3 personas in the deck — flag which slide matters most to each in a comment

Context:
Source material is loaded in this project — the founder's one-pager with problem/solution/market/traction/ask, plus 3 investor personas. Use them.

Intent:
A partner at a seed fund sits through the deck in a 30-minute meeting and wants a second conversation. Not because the slides are pretty — because the founder has thought harder about the problem than the partner has.

Don't: pitch in the slides. The talking points are what gets pitched. The slides are anchors.

Go.</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Critique Prompts — pick one</span>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When the insight slide is generic ("AI changes how teams work")</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"Rewrite it to be specific to Pulseboard — the non-obvious thing Marcus knows about engineering leadership that most people don't. Make it sharp."</p>
                </div>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When the market slide uses aspirational TAM</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"Replace it with the serviceable market: ~40,000 mid-market eng orgs, $800/month per team, path to $10M ARR from ~1,000 teams."</p>
                </div>
                <div class="critique-item">
                  <div class="ci-when">When the ask slide is vague ("raising to scale")</div>
                  <p class="ci-quote">"Rewrite with the specific amount ($1.5M seed), what it funds, and the milestone it unlocks. Make it concrete enough a partner could say yes or no from this slide alone."</p>
                </div>
              </div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">What Good Looks Like</span>
                <ul class="checklist">
                  <li>Exactly 10 slides, opening slide introduces the problem, not the company</li>
                  <li>Market slide quantifies the serviceable market, not aspirational TAM</li>
                  <li>Traction leads with the strongest signal (NPS 71, 2 paying)</li>
                  <li>All 3 investor personas' needs are addressed somewhere in the deck</li>
                </ul>
                <div class="done-bar">"If a partner asked me the three hardest questions, <strong>I could answer all three</strong> from what I already know." Not "it sounds good."</div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- PANEL 03 — DASHBOARD -->
      <div class="panel" data-panel="2">
        <div class="wrap" style="padding:0;">
          <div class="persona-head">
            <div>
              <span class="eyebrow on-light"><span class="dot"></span>VP OPS PERSONA</span>
              <h2>VP of Operations — Series B SaaS</h2>
              <p>Six months of mostly-declining metrics. Builds a board-ready HTML dashboard that surfaces bad news first and names owners.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="panel-grid">
            <div>
              <div class="p-card">
                <span class="p-eyebrow">Source Material</span>
                <h3>Q3 board metrics — mostly red</h3>
                <p>MRR, new customers, NRR, gross churn, logo churn, CAC, LTV:CAC, burn, runway, cash — quarterly table (Jul–Sep 2026) trending against targets, plus the CEO's notes on cross-team causes and the Oct 28 board ask.</p>
